From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patrick Rudolph <patrick.rudolph@9elements.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 08:18:32 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] UefiPayloadPkg: UefiPayloadEntry: Fix MMCONF resource type

The Linux kernel expects the MMCONF MMIO space to be marked as
reserved in the e820 / UEFI memory map. Add an exception to make
sure the range is marked as reserved.

The same code can be found on OvmfPkg that marks the MMCONF as
reserved.

TEST=Boot Linux and find the MMCONF MMIO space be marked as reserved.
TEST="fwts mcfg" reports no test failures any more.

Signed-off-by: Patrick Rudolph <patrick.rudolph@9elements.com>

ouroBIOS backport of commit a87a9b251566 of MrChromebox's edk2, which
uefipayload_2608 had on 2026-11-02 and no branch of that repository
has on 2026-11-03: the lines of the patch that GitHub still shows for
that commit. Drop it when the base includes the change; take the new
version if MrChromebox brings it back changed.

Why here: coreboot's memory table gives the ECAM window of both boards
(and of QEMU q35) as reserved, and this callback made it MMIO, which
does not reach the memory map that the OS reads: GetMemoryMap() lists
reserved ranges, and MMIO only with EFI_MEMORY_RUNTIME. "fwts mcfg"
then fails with MCFGMMIONotReserved (HIGH), which CONF-03 of ouroBIOS's
docs/testing.md does not allow. Linux uses the window anyway, from the
MCFG table at its early check, since the BIOS date is 2016 or later.

TEST=made by hand from that patch on the series, whose file differs
from MrChromebox's elsewhere (edk2/0030, 0042): the same 15 and 2
lines, at upstream's lines. The update test of ouroBIOS checks the
window in the memory map of ExitBootServices() in QEMU q35.
---
 .../UefiPayloadEntry/UefiPayloadEntry.c         | 17 +++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/UefiPayloadPkg/UefiPayloadEntry/UefiPayloadEntry.c b/UefiPayloadPkg/UefiPayloadEntry/UefiPayloadEntry.c
index c892b2906..75fd319a5 100644
--- a/UefiPayloadPkg/UefiPayloadEntry/UefiPayloadEntry.c
+++ b/UefiPayloadPkg/UefiPayloadEntry/UefiPayloadEntry.c
@@ -61,9 +61,22 @@ MemInfoCallbackMmio (
 
   if (MemoryMapEntry->Base == AcpiBoardInfo->PcieBaseAddress) {
     //
-    // MMCONF is always MMIO
+    // Note: there should be an
     //
-    Type = EFI_RESOURCE_MEMORY_MAPPED_IO;
+    //   Type = EFI_RESOURCE_MEMORY_MAPPED_IO;
+    //
+    // asignment below. However, Linux insists that the MMCONFIG area be
+    // marked in the E820 or UEFI memory map as "reserved memory" --
+    // Linux does not content itself with a simple gap
+    // in the memory map wherever the MCFG ACPI table points to.
+    //
+    // This appears to be a safety measure. The PCI Firmware Specification
+    // (rev 3.1) says in 4.1.2. "MCFG Table Description": "The resources can
+    // *optionally* be returned in [...] EFIGetMemoryMap as reserved memory
+    // [...]". (Emphasis added here.)
+    //
+    //
+    Type = EFI_RESOURCE_MEMORY_RESERVED;
   } else if (MemoryMapEntry->Base < mTopOfLowerUsableDram) {
     //
     // It's in DRAM and thus must be reserved
-- 
